# A very important alternative

There is actually a cleaner design if your first IDoc already has an appropriate **customer enhancement/user exit/BAdI at the point where the delivery update is being prepared**.

Instead of:

```text
IDoc processing
   ↓
LIKP update
   ↓
Z-table update
```

you can have:

```text
IDoc processing
   ↓
Determine External ID + Delivery
   ↓
Standard LIKP update
   +
Z-table update
   ↓
Same LUW
   ↓
COMMIT
```

This is preferable because the two pieces of persistence are transactionally coupled.

---

# What if I can't find such an enhancement?

Then I would **not invent a fake "successful IDoc" check**.

Instead, use the IDoc's own completion status as the trigger.

For example:

```text
External ID IDoc
       |
       v
Standard processing
       |
       v
IDoc status 53?
       |
      YES
       |
       v
Persist External ID
```

But there is a catch.

If you update the Z-table from a **separate program after status 53**, you are now in a different LUW:

```text
LUW 1
IDoc → LIKP → COMMIT
              ↓
           Status 53

LUW 2
Read IDoc → Update Z-table → COMMIT
```

This is still reliable if designed carefully, but it is **eventual consistency**, not atomic consistency.

It also introduces the possibility of:

```text
LIKP committed
IDoc = 53
Z-table update fails
```

So you'd need retry/error handling.

---

# Therefore, my preferred hierarchy is

### 🥇 Option 1 — Same LUW

Best:

```text
IDoc
 ↓
LIKP update
 ↓
Z-table update
 ↓
COMMIT
```

This gives you transactional consistency.

### 🥈 Option 2 — Update-task/customer enhancement

If the standard process uses update tasks, integrate your Z-table persistence into the appropriate update mechanism so that it commits with the delivery update.

### 🥉 Option 3 — Post-success processing

If SAP does not expose a suitable enhancement point:

```text
IDoc → LIKP → commit → status 53
                         ↓
                    post-processing
                         ↓
                     Z-table
```

This is acceptable, but requires retry/error monitoring.

